is phase before  coronavirus the largest one-week job  loss new unemployment claims  was somewhere in the 600,000 range maybe  close to 700,000 we have done five to  ten times those numbers not in total  during coronavirus each week during the  last seven weeks and again the total  number is now thirty three and a half  million jobs lost now there are people  who are seeing these numbers and they're  in the camp of open it all right now no  matter what to save the economy right  there they're in that camp and those  folks see these numbers and they say  here's the evidence here's the evidence  that we must reopen everything right now  regardless of the human cost and by the  way the human cost is really big anyway  with things shut down which is true and  that tells us just how contagious  coronaviruses that's a very  short-sighted perspective it's not a  good idea but I do believe that there  are things that we can glean from these  numbers that should inform what should  have been done and also how things  should be handled going forward first of  all even economies that appear to be  doing well Trump has called the economy  up until seven weeks ago the best  economy in the history of any country  ever I don't call it that but there's no  doubt that what we had up until seven  weeks ago was an economy that by the  normal metrics we use unemployment  inflation consumer confidence stock  market all of those measures it was  doing well it was an economy that was  doing well but what we learned is even  those economies are extremely fragile  even those economies can on the drop of  a dime make a hundred and eighty degree  turn this fragility is inherent to our  economy in part because of the marginal  nature of a lot of the work in the  economy in part because of the low  savings rate which is fueled by stagnant  wages over a long period of time  dramatic inequality also gives you  average --is that seem okay even when a  lot of individuals are in a very  precarious f